      "title": "The adjective changes with the noun",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "An adjective describes a noun, like good, nice, new, or old. In Norwegian, many adjectives change form to match the noun they describe."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "A noun can be common-gender, such as "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "en bil",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": ", or neuter, such as "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "et hus",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": ". A noun can also be singular, for one thing, or plural, for more than one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "rule",
          "statement": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": "Before a noun with a or an in English, use the base adjective for common-gender singular, add "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "-t",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " for neuter singular, and add "
            },
            {
              "kind": "foreign_term",
              "value": "-e",
              "lang": "no"
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "value": " for plural."
            }
          ]
